tags: arc:conductor-input, conductor, voice, gaze, camera, whisperkit, bugfix, architecture
body: |-
  ## Conductor v0.5.0–v0.5.2 Session

  ### Architecture Change: Shared Camera
  The original architecture had MediaPipeGazeProvider running a Python subprocess with OpenCV camera access, while VisionGestureProvider ran its own AVCaptureSession. These conflicted because macOS only allows one active capture session per camera device.

  Solution: `SharedCameraSession` — a single AVCaptureSession that distributes `CVPixelBuffer` frames to any number of `CameraFrameConsumer` protocol conformers. Both VisionGazeProvider and VisionGestureProvider register as consumers and run their own Vision requests independently on the same frames.

  ### Native Gaze: VisionGazeProvider
  Replaced the Python/MediaPipe subprocess with native Apple Vision `VNDetectFaceLandmarksRequest`. Extracts pupil positions (with eye center fallback for macOS versions where `leftPupil`/`rightPupil` are nil) and eyebrow distances. Feeds into existing GazeCalibration + KalmanFilter2D pipeline.

  ### Voice: Continuous Mode + Token Filtering
  WhisperVoiceProvider was push-to-talk only (required eyebrow raise trigger). Added continuous recording mode: 4-second audio chunks auto-transcribed and yielded to the buffer. On stop, remaining audio is flushed for one final transcription.

  WhisperKit outputs bracketed tokens for non-speech audio (`[BLANK_AUDIO]`, `[inaudible]`, `[NOISE]`, etc.). Initially used a hardcoded list which missed lowercase variants. Switched to generic bracket stripping that removes any `[...]` pattern.

  ### Dispatch Fix: Zone Router Target
  The Send button was disabled because `BufferView` only checked `gazeRouter.currentTarget`. With workspace-managed instances, targeting happens via `GazeZoneRouter` which sets `targetedInstance` (a `ManagedInstance`). The zone router does call `GazeRouter.shared.setTarget()` for backward compat, but `ManagedInstance.instance` can be nil before AX detection, leaving the gaze router target nil. Fixed by checking both target sources in the disable condition and in `dispatchToTarget()`.
